WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.816
Much federal reasoning runs two questions
together: GCC is Moderate, Moderate

2
00:00:04.816 --> 00:00:09.288
is why the feature is missing, and a
High certification would bring it back.

3
00:00:09.288 --> 00:00:11.787
The Marketplace doesn't
support that chain.

4
00:00:11.787 --> 00:00:19.099
Read on October 21, 2026, it lists
three Microsoft 365 government packages.

5
00:00:19.099 --> 00:00:23.673
The GCC package agencies run is
FedRAMP Certified at Class C,

6
00:00:23.673 --> 00:00:26.325
Moderate, with 100 authorizations.

7
00:00:26.325 --> 00:00:31.775
A second package with the identical name
is In Process at Class D, High, with none.

8
00:00:31.775 --> 00:00:37.025
And GCC High is a separate package,
certified at Class D, with 5.

9
00:00:37.025 --> 00:00:41.520
The straightforward reading of that
second package is a High effort for GCC,

10
00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.588
but the Marketplace doesn't
state how the two relate,

11
00:00:44.588 --> 00:00:46.800
or whether one will replace the other.

12
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.975
Treat it as a question for
the provider, not a roadmap.

13
00:00:49.975 --> 00:00:53.693
Microsoft's own GCC service
description adds a wrinkle:

14
00:00:53.693 --> 00:00:58.381
it says Office 365 Government supports
FedRAMP at a High impact level,

15
00:00:58.381 --> 00:01:01.210
while the Marketplace record says Class C.

16
00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:05.575
Cite the Marketplace and the
package, and record the discrepancy.

17
00:01:05.575 --> 00:01:06.763
Now the key point.

18
00:01:06.763 --> 00:01:13.488
Network connectivity is missing in GCC
High too, and GCC High is already Class D.

19
00:01:13.488 --> 00:01:16.837
A higher class changes what data
you may place in the service.

20
00:01:16.837 --> 00:01:20.950
It doesn't, by itself, restore
a feature the provider excluded.

21
00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:24.445
An agency that defers an equivalent
because High is coming may

22
00:01:24.445 --> 00:01:28.075
wait for something the certification
was never going to deliver.

23
00:01:28.075 --> 00:01:31.488
So plan against the class of
the package you actually use.

24
00:01:31.488 --> 00:01:35.303
And don't hard-code Moderate into
a multi-year architecture either,

25
00:01:35.303 --> 00:01:37.037
because that premise may move.

26
00:01:37.037 --> 00:01:39.637
The sponsor and CIO own that honest plan,

27
00:01:39.637 --> 00:01:43.080
in the current architecture
and acquisition cycle.
